WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.728
Budgets and alerts, in layers.

2
00:00:02.008 --> 00:00:06.744
A budget is only useful if something enforces it and someone hears when it trips.

3
00:00:07.024 --> 00:00:11.717
Think in layers, each catching a different failure: Per response: max_tokens.

4
00:00:11.997 --> 00:00:21.704
A backstop against a runaway generation, set generously (the cost guide suggests 64,000 for agentic work, with streaming).

5
00:00:21.984 --> 00:00:23.669
It caps damage, not spend.

6
00:00:23.949 --> 00:00:27.384
Per task: your own loop guard, plus an advisory budget.

7
00:00:27.664 --> 00:00:42.149
In an agent loop, accumulate the cost of each response as it arrives and stop (or escalate to a person) when a task crosses a dollar limit you chose from the observed distribution, such as a multiple of the 95th percentile.

8
00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:52.883
Anthropic also offers task budgets in beta, which show the model a token budget so it can pace itself; the docs describe them as advisory, so keep your hard guard.

9
00:00:53.163 --> 00:00:55.360
Per workspace or key: spend limits.

10
00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.395
The Claude Console’s workspace spend limit is the final backstop.

11
00:00:59.675 --> 00:01:09.125
Separate workspaces or API keys per product or environment are also what make spend attributable, because a shared key blends everyone’s traffic.

12
00:01:09.405 --> 00:01:11.603
Alerts on rates, not only totals.

13
00:01:11.883 --> 00:01:14.635
A monthly total alert fires after the damage.

14
00:01:14.915 --> 00:01:22.701
Alert on cost per completed task, on the share of tasks hitting your per-task guard, and on the cache read fraction (lesson 5).

15
00:01:22.981 --> 00:01:25.925
Each of those moves within hours of a bad deploy.

16
00:01:26.205 --> 00:01:28.744
The checks-and-balances habit applies here too.

17
00:01:29.024 --> 00:01:39.797
A budget guard is code that changes what users get, so test it: replay a logged runaway task and confirm the guard stops it, and confirm a normal task doesn’t trip it.
